RISE ASCII SCENE 7
#############################################################################################################
#                                      RISE Ascii Scene File                                                #
#                                                                                                           #
#   File:           variety_gallery.RISEscene                                                               #
#   Description:    THE HAIR / FUR VARIETY PLATE -- nine IDENTICAL milliner's blocks, each wearing a        #
#                   different groom, under ONE unchanged light.  Where cottontail_dusk.RISEscene puts the   #
#                   whole system on a single subject, this file does the opposite: it holds everything      #
#                   constant EXCEPT the groom, so that every difference you see is `hair_geometry` and      #
#                   `hair_material` and nothing else.  Same form, same key, same rim, same fill, same       #
#                   camera, same exposure -- nine answers.                                                  #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   THE CONTROL.  `geo_bust` is one `lathe_geometry`: a turned wooden wig block, 0.31 m     #
#                   tall, revolved from a twenty-point profile (base disc, shoulder swell, collar cut,      #
#                   neck, domed skull).  It is deliberately rotationally symmetric and featureless -- a     #
#                   milliner's block IS a surface of revolution, so the "identical forms" premise is        #
#                   satisfied BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by care, and there is no face to distract from    #
#                   the fibre.  Nine `standard_object`s share that single geometry; only the groom bound    #
#                   alongside each one differs.                                                             #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   THE NINE, left to right by row, front row nearest:                                      #
#                                                                                                           #
#                     front   S1 golden blond bob      S2 jet black straight    S3 copper ringlets          #
#                     middle  S4 afro-textured coils   S5 silver-grey (Tier 2)  S6 fox two-layer coat       #
#                     back    S7 angora undercoat      S8 coarse boar bristle   S9 sheep fleece (Tier 3)    #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   They are ordered for ADJACENCY CONTRAST, not for taxonomy: blond sits beside jet        #
#                   black so the multiple-scattering glow of low melanin reads against the near-total       #
#                   absorption of high melanin; the coils sit beside the silver; the fleece sits beside     #
#                   the bristle, soft mass against wire.                                                     #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   ALL THREE COLOUR TIERS ARE ON THE PLATE.  `hair_material` accepts exactly one of        #
#                   three ways to say what a fibre is made of, and this scene uses all three so they can    #
#                   be compared under one light:  Tier 1 (`eumelanin` / `pheomelanin`, the physically       #
#                   based default) on seven specimens -- the whole human range from 0.28 eumelanin at S1    #
#                   to 5.5 at S4 comes out of that one pair of numbers;  Tier 2 (`sigma_a`, direct          #
#                   per-channel absorption) on S5, which is how you ask for a genuinely ACHROMATIC fibre    #
#                   -- 0.150 / 0.156 / 0.175 is flat grey in a way no melanin pair can be, since both       #
#                   pigments are coloured;  Tier 3 (`color`, artist reflectance, inverted per Chiang's      #
#                   sigma_a = (ln C / D(beta_n))^2) on S9, the fleece, where "pale cream wool" is a         #
#                   swatch and not a chemistry.                                                             #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   MEDULLA IS THE HAIR / FUR AXIS.  `medulla_ratio` 0 (S1, S2, S3 -- human hair) gives     #
#                   the plain Chiang model, thin and shiny.  Turning it up splits TT and TRT into           #
#                   unscattered and medulla-scattered halves (Yan et al. 2017), which is what makes a       #
#                   coat read as soft and saturated instead: S6's undercoat at 0.80, S9's fleece at 0.72,   #
#                   and S7's angora at 0.90 -- the highest on the plate, and the specimen that reads as     #
#                   velvet rather than as strands.                                                          #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   TWO COVERAGE FIELDS, SHARED BY ALL NINE.  `pnt_scalp` is a hairline: a smoothstep on    #
#                   `Po.y + 0.55 * (-Po.z)`, so hair stops on the front of the skull and runs down to the   #
#                   nape at the back.  `pnt_pelt` is the animal answer -- everything above the base disc.   #
#                   The five HAIR specimens take the hairline, the four FUR specimens take the pelt, and    #
#                   that single choice is most of why the two families read as different KINDS of thing     #
#                   and not just different colours.  Both are `scalar_painter { expression ... }` over      #
#                   the base's own object space: a groom's `density` and `length_painter` are evaluated     #
#                   at each candidate ROOT during `HairGeometry::Realize()`, which runs once per            #
#                   GEOMETRY and knows nothing of any `standard_object` transform -- so `Po` is the base    #
#                   geometry's local position, and one painter is correct for all nine placements at all    #
#                   three tier heights.                                                                     #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   HONEST LIMITATION, MEASURED HERE: `gravity` IS THE ONLY FLOW LEVER ON A SURFACE OF      #
#                   REVOLUTION.  `comb` is a tangent-space field in the frame {dP/du, bitangent, normal},   #
#                   and on a lathe (UV = angle, arc length along the profile) -- as on the cylindrical      #
#                   wrap of an `sdf_geometry` -- dP/du is AZIMUTHAL.  A constant comb painter therefore     #
#                   sweeps hair AROUND the form, which renders as a lateral flare and a part, not as a      #
#                   sweep down the head.  This was A/B'd on both base kinds during look-dev: comb up,       #
#                   comb down and no comb all flare; only `gravity` (world -Y, frame-independent) drapes.   #
#                   `hair_guides` IS the way out, and S5 is the proof -- CORRECTING what an earlier         #
#                   draft of this header asserted here.  A guide is indeed replayed in the same surface     #
#                   frame, but a comb is ONE VECTOR and a guide is a WHOLE CURVE: the frame fixes only      #
#                   where the curve is aimed, never what shape it has, so an S-bend authored through a      #
#                   guide's points survives the transport intact and says what no constant comb can.        #
#                   Every OTHER draped specimen here still gets its fall from `gravity` alone, and S1's     #
#                   asymmetric sweep is comb used FOR what it actually does (`0.80 0.5 0.5`, an             #
#                   azimuthal push that reads as a side part).  What the frame DOES still cost a guide      #
#                   set is written up at S5.                                                                #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   THE RIG, and why it is three lights and not one.  A single instrument cannot show a     #
#                   fibre BCSDF: the R lobe wants a source in front, the TT lobe wants one behind, and      #
#                   the multiple-scattering glow of a pale groom only separates from a dark one when the    #
#                   shadow side is open enough to have colour in it.  `key` is a warm 1.7 m panel high on   #
#                   the left (form and the anisotropic sheen band).  `rim` is a 4.2 m wide panel behind     #
#                   and above the whole array, out of frame, aimed forward and down -- that is the TT       #
#                   halo, and it is what makes S8's quills legible and S2's black hair glow red at its      #
#                   edge instead of dying into the backdrop.  `fill` is a dim cool panel on the right so    #
#                   the unlit halves stay chromatic.  The room is deliberately near-black (floor 0.007,     #
#                   backdrop 0.010, blocks 0.013): the specimens are the only bright things in the frame.   #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   STAGING.  Three tiers at y = 0.00 / 0.46 / 0.92 on `lathe_geometry` stems, each row     #
#                   0.78 m further back and STAGGERED half a column sideways so the stems never line up     #
#                   into vertical totem poles.  The camera is a 245 mm lens 13 m away -- near enough to     #
#                   orthographic that the back row is only ~12 % smaller than the front, which is what      #
#                   lets a three-deep arrangement read as a plate of nine equal specimens rather than as    #
#                   a receding crowd.  f/11 keeps all three tiers inside the depth of field.                #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   RENDER SETTINGS.  `oidn_denoise FALSE`, for the reason measured in                      #
#                   docs/HAIR_FUR_DESIGN.md section 6.5: the denoiser removes real strand structure from    #
#                   a groom and the deficit does not close with sample count.  Samples carry the            #
#                   convergence instead.  A groom at these fibre widths (0.09-0.24 mm) is sub-pixel at      #
#                   this framing, so what converges is the MASS -- below ~64 spp every specimen reads as    #
#                   glitter rather than as hair, and the difference between 64 and 256 is the difference    #
#                   between a texture and a material.                                                       #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   COST.  ~1.79 M strands across the nine grooms (S2 and S4 tie as the heaviest at         #
#                   300 k, S8 the lightest at 14 k -- a bristle brush is SPARSE, and that is the point      #
#                   of it).  1400x1050 at 96 spp is 5 min 12 s on a 14-core Apple Silicon laptop, and       #
#                   the committed 256 spp scales to ~14 min.  Drop to 48 spp for look-dev: composition,     #
#                   groom silhouettes and the colour ordering all read there.                               #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   WHAT STILL FALLS SHORT.  S2 and S5 were re-worked after the first pass, and this        #
#                   is where each of them stands now, measured rather than hoped:                           #
#                                                                                                           #
#                     S2  IMPROVED, NOT CLOSED.  It reads as a glossy black SHELL now -- a hard white       #
#                         crown highlight off the rim, and a satin band down the right flank -- where       #
#                         before it was a matte bell wearing scattered glitter.  But the band is SOFT,      #
#                         not the hard mirror line real jet-black hair shows, and the reason is             #
#                         geometric and beyond the groom's reach: for a fibre hanging vertically the        #
#                         key sits 36.2 deg above that fibre's normal plane and the camera 1.9 deg          #
#                         below it, so the R lobe misses its mirror condition by 38 deg against a           #
#                         beta_m of 6.3 deg.  Closing that would take the fibre tilted ~21 deg off          #
#                         vertical across a wide area, or a cuticle `alpha` near 19 -- five times any       #
#                         real hair.  What IS on the plate is the part of that condition a draped           #
#                         shell's own curvature genuinely crosses, and nothing has been faked to            #
#                         widen it.                                                                         #
#                                                                                                           #
#                     S5  FIXED.  The waves are waves, they come from `hair_guides` -- which nothing        #
#                         else in the repository showcases -- and the featureless luminous mass is          #
#                         gone.  Residual, honestly: the crown is smoother than the fall, because a         #
#                         guide's wobble is authored to grow from zero at the root so that no guide         #
#                         starts inside the block; and S5 is still the brightest thing in the frame,        #
#                         which is what a 0.15 sigma_a under this key is entitled to be.                    #
#                                                                                                           #
#   Author:         RISE contributors                                                                       #
#   Birthdate:      2026-08-28                                                                              #
#                                                                                                           #
#############################################################################################################

standard_shader
{
	name			global
	shaderop		DefaultDirectLighting
}

scene_options
{
	scene_unit		1.0
}

film
{
	width			1400
	height			1050
}

thinlens_camera
{
	location		0.00 0.615 13.00
	lookat			0.00 0.615 -0.78
	up			0 1 0
	sensor_size		36.0
	focal_length		245.0
	fstop			11.0
	focus_distance		13.78
	aperture_blades		9
}

pathtracing_pel_rasterizer
{
	samples			256
	rr_min_depth		6
	oidn_denoise		FALSE
}

file_rasterizeroutput
{
	pattern			rendered/variety_gallery
	type			PNG
	bpp			8
	color_space		sRGB
}

##################################################
# LIGHT.  Three panels, unchanged for all nine.
#
# `key`   -- warm, high left, in front: form + the R-lobe sheen band.
# `rim`   -- a 4.2 m panel behind and above the whole array, aimed
#            forward and down and well out of frame.  This is the TT
#            lobe's instrument: light that entered one side of a fibre,
#            crossed its interior and left the other side.  It is the
#            single most expensive thing a hair BCSDF buys, and every
#            specimen on the plate is posed to show it.
# `fill`  -- dim and cool, right: keeps the shadow halves chromatic so
#            a pale groom's multiple-scattering glow can be told apart
#            from a dark groom's absorption.
#
# Exitances are balanced so the BRIGHTEST specimen (S5 / S7) sits just
# under clipping; everything else is measured against that.
##################################################
rect_light
{
	name			key
	center			-1.55 1.85 1.55
	size			1.70 1.70
	facing			0.50 -0.40 -0.77
	color			1.00 0.88 0.72
	exitance		210.0
}

rect_light
{
	name			rim
	center			0.00 2.55 -2.90
	size			4.20 1.10
	facing			0.00 -0.56 0.83
	color			1.00 0.95 0.90
	exitance		215.0
}

rect_light
{
	name			fill
	center			2.20 0.95 1.85
	size			2.20 1.70
	facing			-0.62 -0.10 -0.76
	color			0.66 0.74 1.00
	exitance		24.0
}

##################################################
# ROOM.  Near-black, on purpose.  The specimens are the story, so the
# floor is 0.007, the backdrop 0.010 and the blocks 0.013 -- all of
# them an order of magnitude below the grooms they carry.
##################################################
uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			pnt_floor
	color			0.007 0.008 0.010
}

orennayar_material
{
	name			mat_floor
	reflectance		pnt_floor
	roughness		0.85
}

infiniteplane_geometry
{
	name			geo_floor
	xtile			1.0
	ytile			1.0
}

standard_object
{
	name			obj_floor
	geometry		geo_floor
	material		mat_floor
	position		0 0 0
	orientation		-90 0 0
}

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			pnt_wall
	color			0.010 0.011 0.014
}

orennayar_material
{
	name			mat_wall
	reflectance		pnt_wall
	roughness		0.90
}

infiniteplane_geometry
{
	name			geo_wall
	xtile			1.0
	ytile			1.0
}

standard_object
{
	name			obj_wall
	geometry		geo_wall
	material		mat_wall
	position		0 0 -4.20
}

##################################################
# THE FORM -- one turned milliner's block, used nine times.
#
# `lathe_geometry` is the right chunk for this because a wig block IS a
# silhouette spun about an axis, and because rotational symmetry makes
# "all nine forms are identical" true by construction rather than by
# care.  Twenty profile points: base disc, bevel, shoulder swell, the
# collar cut, a slim neck, and a domed skull that peaks at r = 0.073
# and closes to a pole at h = 0.310.  `smooth TRUE` averages each
# profile point's two adjacent segment normals, so the turned surface
# shades as a turned surface and not as a stack of cones.
#
# The two stems are the same chunk again, at the two riser heights.
##################################################
uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			pnt_stone
	color			0.0128 0.0133 0.0150
}

orennayar_material
{
	name			mat_stone
	reflectance		pnt_stone
	roughness		0.70
}

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			pnt_column
	color			0.006 0.006 0.007
}

orennayar_material
{
	name			mat_column
	reflectance		pnt_column
	roughness		0.80
}

lathe_geometry
{
	name			geo_bust
	axis			y
	n_radial		96
	smooth			TRUE
	profile_point		0.000 0.000
	profile_point		0.098 0.000
	profile_point		0.098 0.014
	profile_point		0.086 0.026
	profile_point		0.084 0.050
	profile_point		0.096 0.082
	profile_point		0.108 0.104
	profile_point		0.100 0.122
	profile_point		0.070 0.138
	profile_point		0.036 0.152
	profile_point		0.030 0.168
	profile_point		0.032 0.186
	profile_point		0.046 0.198
	profile_point		0.062 0.212
	profile_point		0.072 0.234
	profile_point		0.073 0.252
	profile_point		0.066 0.272
	profile_point		0.050 0.290
	profile_point		0.028 0.302
	profile_point		0.000 0.310
}

lathe_geometry
{
	name			geo_col_b
	axis			y
	n_radial		48
	smooth			TRUE
	profile_point		0.000 0.000
	profile_point		0.076 0.000
	profile_point		0.076 0.018
	profile_point		0.028 0.036
	profile_point		0.022 0.434
	profile_point		0.058 0.446
	profile_point		0.058 0.460
	profile_point		0.000 0.460
}

lathe_geometry
{
	name			geo_col_c
	axis			y
	n_radial		48
	smooth			TRUE
	profile_point		0.000 0.000
	profile_point		0.080 0.000
	profile_point		0.080 0.020
	profile_point		0.028 0.038
	profile_point		0.022 0.894
	profile_point		0.058 0.906
	profile_point		0.058 0.920
	profile_point		0.000 0.920
}

##################################################
# SHARED GROOM FIELDS -- the same four painters for all nine specimens.
#
# `pnt_scalp` is a HAIRLINE.  The smoothstep runs on `Po.y + 0.55 *
# (-Po.z)`, a plane tilted back over the skull, so growth stops across
# the front of the head and carries on down to the nape behind.  The
# five HAIR specimens use it.
#
# `pnt_pelt` is the animal answer: everything above the base disc, so
# the coat covers the whole block the way a pelt covers a whole animal.
# The four FUR specimens use it.  That one binding is most of why the
# two families read as different KINDS of thing rather than as
# recolourings of each other.
#
# `Po` here is the BASE GEOMETRY'S OWN object space.  A groom is
# realized once per `hair_geometry`, from the base's tessellation, with
# no `standard_object` transform in scope -- so one painter is correct
# for every placement at every tier height, and moving a specimen never
# moves its hairline.
#
# `pnt_part` is a comb field, and it is the honest use of one on a
# surface of revolution: `0.80 0.5 0.5` is d = (0.6, 0, 0) along the
# AZIMUTHAL tangent, which reads as a side part and a sweep around the
# head.  It cannot sweep hair DOWN the head -- see the header's
# limitation note.  Only S1 uses it.
##################################################
scalar_painter
{
	name			pnt_scalp
	def			hl smoothstep(0.168, 0.215, Po.y + 0.55 * (0.0 - Po.z))
	def			n  0.5 + 0.5 * fbm(Po * 62.0, 3.0, 0.5, 2.0)
	expression		hl * clamp(0.74 + 0.36 * n, 0.0, 1.0)
}

scalar_painter
{
	name			pnt_pelt
	def			top smoothstep(0.048, 0.086, Po.y)
	def			n   0.5 + 0.5 * fbm(Po * 55.0, 3.0, 0.5, 2.0)
	expression		top * clamp(0.76 + 0.34 * n, 0.0, 1.0)
}

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name			pnt_part
	color			0.80 0.5 0.5
}

scalar_painter
{
	name			pnt_lenbob
	def			n 0.5 + 0.5 * fbm(Po * 55.0, 3.0, 0.5, 2.0)
	expression		0.93 + 0.14 * clamp(n, 0.0, 1.0)
}

scalar_painter
{
	name			pnt_lenjit
	def			n 0.5 + 0.5 * fbm(Po * 40.0, 3.0, 0.5, 2.0)
	expression		0.78 + 0.42 * clamp(n, 0.0, 1.0)
}

##################################################
# S1 -- GOLDEN BLOND BOB.  The multiple-scattering specimen.
#
# Eumelanin 0.28 with pheomelanin 0.42 is the low-absorption end of the
# human range, and it is the one groom on the plate where light entering
# the mass genuinely bounces fibre-to-fibre before it leaves.  Put it
# next to S2 (eumelanin 4.5) under the same rim and the difference is
# not a hue shift, it is a change of KIND: the blond glows from within
# its own volume, the black returns only its surface lobes.
#
# `comb pnt_part` is the plate's one comb, used for what a comb can
# actually do on a lathe -- an azimuthal push that reads as a side part.
##################################################
scalar_painter
{
	name			eu_s1
	value			0.28
}

scalar_painter
{
	name			ph_s1
	value			0.42
}

hair_material
{
	name			mat_s1
	eumelanin		eu_s1
	pheomelanin		ph_s1
	beta_m			0.28
	beta_n			0.34
	alpha			3.0
	ior			1.55
}

hair_geometry
{
	name			groom_s1
	base_geometry		geo_bust
	count			240000
	segments		12
	length			0.100
	width_root		0.00009
	width_tip		0.00004
	base_detail		144
	seed			11
	density			pnt_scalp
	comb			pnt_part
	length_painter		pnt_lenbob
	gravity			0.95
	frizz			0.07
	clump			0.22
	clump_size		0.012
	curl_radius		0.006
	curl_step		0.100
}

##################################################
# S2 -- JET BLACK STRAIGHT.  The sheen specimen.
#
# High eumelanin, no medulla, and the LOWEST roughness pair on the
# plate (beta_m 0.11 / beta_n 0.12, against S8's 0.20 / 0.22 next) --
# a narrow, hard, anisotropic R lobe, which is what "glossy black
# hair" means physically.  At this absorption almost everything that
# enters a fibre stays there, so to a camera in FRONT of the specimen
# the R lobe is very nearly all of it -- what TT survives shows up only
# as the red edge the back panel lights, and never on the form itself.
# The groom below is therefore built for one lobe and one lobe only.
#
# WHICH IS WHY IT IS A SHELL AND NOT A MOP.  `frizz` 0.000 and `clump`
# 0.08 are the two lowest on the plate, and deliberately: a mirror cone
# is only legible while it stays COHERENT between neighbouring fibres,
# and jitter of either kind decorrelates it into glitter -- which is
# precisely what the first pass of this specimen produced.  `gravity`
# 0.72 rather than the 1.00 that pass used, because a strand which
# snaps vertical in its first centimetre offers one tangent direction
# and one only, whereas a slower drape sweeps its tangent smoothly
# through a range, and the sheen IS the locus where that sweep crosses
# the mirror condition.  The fibres are widened to 0.17 mm so the lobe
# has per-fibre area to sit on, and there are 300 k of them so the
# shell stays opaque enough to read as a surface rather than a cloud.
#
# WHERE THE LIGHT ACTUALLY LANDS, measured by switching the other two
# panels off in a draft copy: the key returns a mean of 3.98/255 over
# the specimen, concentrated in the right third (7.0 there against 2.7
# on the left); the fill returns 0.09 and is not a factor at all.  The
# white patch on the crown is the RIM's R lobe, not the key's -- hair
# rolling over the top of the dome is the one place on this block
# whose tangents can answer a source that far behind it.  See the
# header's WHAT STILL FALLS SHORT for what this specimen does not do.
##################################################
scalar_painter
{
	name			eu_s2
	value			4.50
}

scalar_painter
{
	name			ph_s2
	value			0.15
}

hair_material
{
	name			mat_s2
	eumelanin		eu_s2
	pheomelanin		ph_s2
	beta_m			0.110
	beta_n			0.120
	alpha			2.6
	ior			1.55
}

hair_geometry
{
	name			groom_s2
	base_geometry		geo_bust
	count			300000
	segments		16
	length			0.185
	width_root		0.00017
	width_tip		0.00010
	base_detail		144
	seed			23
	density			pnt_scalp
	length_painter		pnt_lenbob
	gravity			0.72
	frizz			0.000
	clump			0.08
	clump_size		0.030
}

##################################################
# S3 -- COPPER RINGLETS.  The pheomelanin specimen.
#
# Pheomelanin 4.60 against eumelanin 0.95: red hair is not brown hair
# with a red tint, it is a different pigment dominating, and the tier-1
# parameterisation says so directly.  `curl_radius` 0.0125 with
# `curl_step` 0.030 -- a helix whose pitch is a quarter of its own
# length -- is a ringlet; raise the step toward the strand length (see
# S5) and the same two numbers give a lazy wave instead.  18 control
# points, because a tight helix on 8 is a polygon.
##################################################
scalar_painter
{
	name			eu_s3
	value			0.95
}

scalar_painter
{
	name			ph_s3
	value			4.60
}

hair_material
{
	name			mat_s3
	eumelanin		eu_s3
	pheomelanin		ph_s3
	beta_m			0.30
	beta_n			0.32
	alpha			3.0
	ior			1.55
}

hair_geometry
{
	name			groom_s3
	base_geometry		geo_bust
	count			220000
	segments		18
