	clump_size		0.010
	curl_radius		0.0045
	curl_step		0.011
}

##################################################
# S5 -- SILVER-GREY WAVES.  The Tier-2 specimen, the achromatic one,
# and the plate's one GUIDED groom.
#
# `sigma_a` binds absorption per RGB channel DIRECTLY, with no melanin
# model in between -- which is the only way to ask for a genuinely
# neutral fibre, since eumelanin and pheomelanin are both coloured
# pigments and no mixture of them is flat grey.  0.150 / 0.156 / 0.175
# is a hair's breadth cool, which is what un-pigmented keratin actually
# looks like next to a warm key.
#
# THE WAVES ARE `hair_guides`, AND THEY HAVE TO BE.  The first pass
# asked `curl_radius` 0.014 / `curl_step` 0.050 for them and got a
# luminous featureless mass instead, which is the correct outcome and
# not a bug: a curl is a HELIX of fixed radius about the strand's own
# axis, and a wave is a low-frequency undulation OF that axis.  Open
# the pitch far enough that the helix stops reading as a ringlet and
# what is left is a straight strand -- there is no setting of the two
# numbers that is a wave.  `frizz` cannot supply one either; noise is
# not undulation.  `gd_wave` below states the shape outright.
#
# THE GUIDE SET, and why it is shaped the way it is.  37 curves on four
# rings of the skull (5 at the crown, 10 / 14 over the dome, 8 across
# the nape, where the hairline field actually grows hair).  Each one is
# INTEGRATED rather than plotted: it leaves along the local surface
# normal and turns toward -Y as it goes, so a crown guide arcs OVER the
# dome instead of diving through it -- an earlier set that simply fell
# from its root left the crown bare, because on the top of a head
# "straight down" is "into the block".  On top of that spine rides an
# S-wave, 1.65 periods of tangential swing and 1.15 of radial, its
# amplitude ramped from zero at the root so no guide ever starts inside
# the block.  Phase advances once around the head per ring and is
# offset between rings, so crests SPIRAL instead of ringing up into
# hoops.  Then `clump` 0.52 lets neighbouring strands undulate together
# as locks (0.36 was tried and mushes the crests out; 0.50 with a
# tighter cell reads as fingers), and the fibre count comes DOWN to
# 88 k at 0.20 mm -- the fewest strands of any hair specimen here --
# because a wave is GEOMETRY and geometry needs to be resolvable, not
# averaged away inside a dense mass.  That drop is also what stopped
# this specimen clipping: median luminance falls from 183/255 to 143.
#
# WHAT THE FRAME COSTS, since the header sends you here.  The transport
# is rigid in the base's {tangent, bitangent, normal} frame, so a guide
# authored in WORLD space is not replayed in world space -- it is
# replayed rotated with the frame.  A version of this set that wobbled
# every guide along world +X (to get undulation into the silhouette at
# the left and right edges, where a radial wobble is invisible edge-on)
# came back MORE homogeneous, not less: azimuthally-rotated copies of
# one world shape interpolate to mush.  Authoring in the polar frame,
# as below, is the honest way to talk to a lathe.
##################################################
hair_guides
